  "prediction": "The Atomic Energy Act (AEA) of 1954, as amended, requires a peaceful nuclear cooperation agreement for all significant U.S. nuclear cooperation with other countries. This includes the transfer of U.S.-origin special nuclear material and the export of reactors and critical parts of reactors. Section 123 agreements, which are congressional-executive agreements, set the terms of reference and authorize cooperation but do not guarantee it. The AEA includes requirements for an agreement's content, conditions for presidential exemptions, and procedures for congressional approval. Section 123 agreements must meet nine nonproliferation criteria and be submitted to Congress with a Nuclear Proliferation Assessment Statement (NPAS) explaining how the agreement meets these requirements. The President must also make a determination that the agreement will promote common defense and security. The AEA provides different requirements, conditions, and procedures for exempt and nonexempt agreements. To date, all Section 123 agreements in force are nonexempt. The AEA also sets out procedures for licensing exports to states with which the U.S. has nuclear cooperation agreements. Each export of nuclear material, equipment, or technology requires a specific export license or other authorization. The Nuclear Regulatory Commission (NRC) is required to meet certain criteria in authorizing export licenses. Recipient states must have adequate physical security on nuclear material and facilities, and nuclear material or equipment cannot be used for nuclear explosive devices or reprocessing without U.S. approval. The AEA allows for limited forms of nuclear cooperation outside of the U.S. for the development or production of special nuclear material outside of the U.S., but only if it is not inimical to U.S. interests. The AEA also includes provisions for the termination of exports to countries that have violated nonproliferation agreements or engaged in activities related to nuclear explosives. Recent legislative proposals have sought to amend the AEA to include additional nonproliferation requirements for nuclear cooperation agreements.",
  "scored_prediction": "The Atomic Energy Act (AEA) of 1954, as amended, requires a peaceful nuclear cooperation agreement for all significant U.S. nuclear cooperation with other countries. This includes the transfer of U.S.-origin special nuclear material and the export of reactors and critical parts of reactors. Section 123 agreements, which are congressional-executive agreements, set the terms of reference and authorize cooperation but do not guarantee it. The AEA includes requirements for an agreement's content, conditions for presidential exemptions, and procedures for congressional approval. Section 123 agreements must meet nine nonproliferation criteria and be submitted to Congress with a Nuclear Proliferation Assessment Statement (NPAS) explaining how the agreement meets these requirements. The President must also make a determination that the agreement will promote common defense and security. The AEA provides different requirements, conditions, and procedures for exempt and nonexempt agreements. To date, all Section 123 agreements in force are nonexempt. The AEA also sets out procedures for licensing exports to states with which the U.S. has nuclear cooperation agreements. Each export of nuclear material, equipment, or technology requires a specific export license or other authorization. The Nuclear Regulatory Commission (NRC) is required to meet certain criteria in authorizing export licenses. Recipient states must have adequate physical security on nuclear material and facilities, and nuclear material or equipment cannot be used for nuclear explosive devices or reprocessing without U.S. approval. The AEA allows for limited forms of nuclear cooperation outside of the U.S. for the development or production of special nuclear material outside of the U.S., but only if it is not inimical to U.S. interests. The AEA also includes provisions for the termination of exports to countries that have violated nonproliferation agreements or engaged in activities related to nuclear explosives. Recent legislative proposals have sought to amend the AEA to include additional nonproliferation requirements for nuclear cooperation agreements.",
  "score": 35.24229033282229,
  "answers": [
    "In order for the United States to engage in significant civilian nuclear cooperation with other states, it must conclude a framework agreement that meets specific requirements under Section 123 of the Atomic Energy Act (AEA). Significant nuclear cooperation includes the export of reactors, critical parts of reactors, and reactor fuel. The AEA also provides for export control licensing procedures and criteria for terminating cooperation. Congressional review is required for Section 123 agreements; the AEA establishes special parliamentary procedures by which Congress may act on a proposed agreement."
  ],
